<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal"
     xmlns:netfx="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/netfx">
  <?include ..\Common\Product.wxi ?>

  <!--
    The NutManager Agent installer, a product separate from the desktop application.

    Unlike the desktop bundle this one has a real prerequisite. The Agent publishes
    framework-dependent, so ASP.NET Core 10 x64 must already be on the machine or be installed here.
    That trade was made on purpose: the Agent is a long-lived service on a server, and a private copy
    of the runtime inside its own directory means a .NET security fix requires a NutManager release
    before that server is patched. Sharing the machine's runtime puts servicing back where the
    administrator already manages it.

    The cost is this chain, and the rule that governs it: never install an Agent that cannot run.
  -->
  <Bundle Name="$(AgentName)"
          Manufacturer="$(Publisher)"
          Version="$(Version)"
          UpgradeCode="$(AgentBundleUpgradeCode)"
          IconSourceFile="$(BrandingIcon)"
          AboutUrl="$(ProjectUrl)"
          Compressed="yes">

    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication Theme="rtfLicense"
                                              ThemeFile="$(ThemeFile)"
                                              LocalizationFile="$(ThemeLocalization)"
                                              LicenseFile="$(TermsFile)"
                                              LogoFile="$(BrandingLogo)"
                                              ShowVersion="yes"
                                              SuppressOptionsUI="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplication>

    <!--
      Asks the question that matters: can this machine run the Agent? DotNetCoreSearch queries the
      installed shared frameworks through the officially supported mechanism rather than looking for
      dotnet.exe on disk or shelling a runtime listing out to the CLI. A file existing is not a
      runtime, and a runtime for the wrong architecture is not a usable one.

      RuntimeType aspnet, not dotnet: the Agent takes a FrameworkReference on Microsoft.AspNetCore.App
      for HTTP.sys, so the base .NET runtime alone would not be enough. Platform x64 because that is
      the only architecture this product ships.

      The variable holds the newest installed 10.x and is left unset when there is none, which is why
      every condition below compares it rather than testing whether it exists.
    -->
    <netfx:DotNetCoreSearch Id="SearchAspNetCore"
                            RuntimeType="aspnet"
                            Platform="x64"
                            MajorVersion="$(AspNetCoreMajorVersion)"
                            Variable="AspNetCoreRuntimeVersion" />

    <!--
      The operator's answer to "shall I install it?", and nothing more.

      Default 1, so the interactive checkbox starts ticked when the runtime is missing and an
      unattended /quiet install on a bare server does the useful thing without an extra switch. It is
      only ever consulted when the runtime is absent - when one is already present the package's
      DetectCondition is satisfied and Burn plans nothing regardless of this value, which is what
      keeps a working server from being handed a runtime it did not need.

      bal:Overridable is what lets an administrator refuse deliberately:

          NutManager-Agent-Setup-x.y.z.exe /quiet InstallAspNetRuntime=0

      With the runtime absent and this set to 0, the install fails before the service is registered.
      That is the intended outcome, not a defect: a NutManagerAgent that installs and then cannot
      start is worse than one that refused, because it leaves something broken behind on a server.
    -->
    <Variable Name="InstallAspNetRuntime" Type="numeric" Value="1" bal:Overridable="yes" />

    <Chain>
      <!--
        The Microsoft runtime, from Microsoft.

        Declaring the payload with a DownloadUrl and no local SourceFile is what makes it remote: it
        stays out of the NutManager download, so the Agent installer remains small and the runtime is
        fetched only when it is actually needed. That URL is fixed at compile time and points at
        Microsoft's own build service - there is no user-supplied URL, no mirror list, no redirect
        through anything this project controls, and no address arriving from an API response.

        Hash and Size are the published values for exactly that build. Burn refuses a payload that
        does not match, so a substituted or corrupted download fails the install instead of silently
        running an unverified executable with elevation.

        Permanent="yes" is the ownership statement: this is a machine-shared Microsoft component and
        removing NutManager Agent must not take it away from whatever else on the server depends on
        it. Uninstall removes what this installer owns, and the runtime is not that.

        Vital="yes", with no retry and no fallback mirror. If the download or the install fails, the
        bundle fails and says so; it does not proceed to lay down an Agent that cannot start.

        InstallCondition is the operator's choice; DetectCondition is the machine's state. Both must
        want it before anything is downloaded.
      -->
      <ExePackage Id="AspNetCoreRuntime"
                  DisplayName="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeDisplayName)"
                  Cache="remove"
                  PerMachine="yes"
                  Permanent="yes"
                  Vital="yes"
                  DetectCondition="AspNetCoreRuntimeVersion &gt;= v$(AspNetCoreMajorVersion).0.0"
                  InstallCondition="InstallAspNetRuntime = 1"
                  InstallArguments="/install /quiet /norestart"
                  RepairArguments="/repair /quiet /norestart"
                  UninstallArguments="/uninstall /quiet /norestart">
        <ExePackagePayload Name="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeFileName)"
                           ProductName="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeDisplayName)"
                           Description="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeDisplayName)"
                           DownloadUrl="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeUrl)"
                           Hash="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeHash)"
                           Size="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeSize)"
                           Version="$(AspNetCoreRuntimeVersion).0" />
      </ExePackage>

      <!--
        The Agent itself, last.

        Its InstallCondition repeats the precondition rather than trusting the disabled button: the
        theme can stop a click, but /quiet has no buttons, and a chain that would register the
        service anyway is one command line away from producing exactly the broken install this whole
        arrangement exists to prevent. Burn evaluates conditions after the prerequisite has run, so a
        runtime installed a moment ago satisfies this.

        ForcePerMachine matches the package's own perMachine scope.
      -->
      <MsiPackage SourceFile="$(PackagePath)"
                  ForcePerMachine="yes"
                  Visible="no"
                  InstallCondition="AspNetCoreRuntimeVersion &gt;= v$(AspNetCoreMajorVersion).0.0 OR InstallAspNetRuntime = 1" />
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
